{"evt":"knowledge_node_upserted","id":"knowledge:source_section:note-mqsi4rz0p98#note-evidence","key":"knowledge:source_section:note-mqsi4rz0p98#note-evidence","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","type":"source_section","kind":"source_section","label":"Bench our-way-prod GPS flake is grader-search variance plus terminate exit-code artifact not a daemon crash evidence","title":"Bench our-way-prod GPS flake is grader-search variance plus terminate exit-code artifact not a daemon crash evidence","summary":"Investigated the agent-memory bench our-way-prod ON-arm flake (answers \"I don't know\" on the LongMemEval Oracle GPS probe; reported as \"embedded daemon exits code=1 mid-drain\" + \"eager judge runs idle, candidates=[]\"). Reproduced from the worktree (HEAD 00806352) with an embedded daemon live-bound to the conv workspace. Two findings:\n\n(1) The \"daemon exits code=1 mid-drain\" is NOT a crash. bench/zonoid_bench/daemon.py stop() calls proc.terminate(), which on Windows is TerminateProcess -> exit code 1. The line \"[daemon.py] daemon pid=X exited with code=1\" is printed by stop() at teardown; piping interleaves stdout/stderr so it APPEARS mid-run. Verified: spawned pid == pidfile pid, /health=ready, Popen.poll()=None (alive) throughout the drain; only after stop() does returncode become 1. So failure (1) is a misread teardown artifact. The embedded daemon does not actually die mid-drain on this box.","metadata":{"note_key":"note:note-mqsi4rz0p98","origin":"overlay/note"},"source_path":null,"section_ref":"note-evidence","chunk_ref":null,"cluster_ref":null,"created_at":"2026-06-24T20:04:19.925Z","updated_at":"2026-06-24T20:04:19.925Z","vec":[-0.08195072412490845,0.007026038598269224,-0.019526738673448563,-0.0026134492363780737,0.069793201982975,-0.09918791800737381,-0.03590870648622513,0.07782862335443497,-0.005851659458130598,0.018147984519600868,-0.056019753217697144,0.08171121031045914,-0.0019215211505070329,-0.04459915682673454,-0.030419332906603813,-0.0267187599092721